# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUN 4 -- the SAME nine arms, rebuilt after the T3.10 triage
#
# WHAT CHANGED IN THE SOURCE, AND WHAT DID NOT. Triage removed dead work from
# impl::BitSlicedPairCounts<N>::add(): it was folding the FULL N x N of xx and yy
# per window row, where bitSlicedPairRowRegion only ever writes the upper triangle
# and combineBitSlicedPairs only ever reads it -- N^2 - N adds per row whose two
# operands were provably both zero (12 of 64 at N = 4). The answers are
# bit-identical by construction, which is what tests/test_covariance.cpp's N = 1..7
# sweeps check; this run is here so that X-22's table describes the kernel that
# actually ships. Nothing else moved, and the window-acc arms are untouched.
#
# AND IT IS NOT A MEASUREMENT OF THAT CHANGE. This is a fourth binary, so the
# ~1.5x code-layout effect above applies to it in full: against run 2 the shipped
# arm is 4.3% FASTER at uint32_t/W=31/N=4 and 4.8% SLOWER at uint32_t/W=31/N=3.
# A dead-work removal cannot be both. Read this run as a re-confirmation of the
# CURVE, not as an A/B -- the A/B that would price the per-row accumulator at all
# is E-13, and it needs a binary per arm.
#
# WHAT IT CONFIRMS. (a) The band-C cells stay where the corrected X-22 puts them:
# W = 7 at N = 2 for both word types (+26%, +30%) and nowhere at W = 31.
# (b) uint64_t at W = 31 is inside the +/-25% band at every N (4.03x / 8.47x /
# 15.18x), as the pre-registered rule says and as the first version of X-22 denied.
# (c) The ternary arm is again SLOWER than the generic bit-sliced arm at N = 1 in
# every one of its six rows, by 3-8% -- a third binary, same direction.
# (d) operator new across 1800 kernel calls per window size: 0, at every N.
#
# Environment: as above; throttled=0x0 before and after.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

